    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear focus on stolen base efficiency and elite top-of-order production. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13) stands as the premier start of the day; his matchup against Brandon Pfaadt is optimized by a slow delivery that promises high-probability theft opportunities, bolstered further by a strong leadoff position. **Yordan Alvarez** (77.56) and **Junior Caminero** (76.90) round out the elite tier, both benefiting from favorable park factors and the reliable run-production potential inherent in their respective #2 and #3 lineup slots. We are heavily leaning into these high-efficiency plays to maintain our 5x5 leverage, with **Lars Nootbaar** (64.56) serving as a critical secondary engine despite facing the tougher Clay Holmes.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the optimizer involved **Christian Encarnacion-Strand**, who finished just outside the starting rotation with a score of 48.22. While he provided a reasonable floor, the optimizer prioritized the specific platoon advantages of **Kazuma Okamoto** (61.52) and the situational upside of **Kyle Schwarber** (59.47) to ensure we maximize our HR and RBI ceilings against arms like Seth Lugo and George Kirby. We remain cautious regarding our catchers, **Gary Sanchez** (55.07) and **Dillon Dingler** (48.56); both occupy the lower end of our starting spectrum, and with their lineups still pending, we are monitoring potential defensive shifts that could further suppress their output. \n\nRisk management remains a priority given the volatility of the \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Gary Sanchez**, **Pedro Ramirez**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, and **Hector Rodriguez**. While **Hector Rodriguez** holds a marginal score of 57.54, he is a primary beneficiary of wind blowing out, providing enough of a ceiling to justify his start despite the unfavorable #9 spot. Conversely, **Christian Yelich** (50.56) remains a watch item; his struggle against left-handed pitching, 